import{_ as t,o as s,c as o,a0 as a}from"./chunks/framework.ChlVJx0I.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"The watcher discovers and the stat check verifies","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"adr/0042-the-watcher-discovers-and-the-stat-check-verifies.md","filePath":"adr/0042-the-watcher-discovers-and-the-stat-check-verifies.md","lastUpdated":1792093740000}'),n={name:"adr/0042-the-watcher-discovers-and-the-stat-check-verifies.md"};function r(c,e,d,i,h,p){return s(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a('<h1 id="the-watcher-discovers-and-the-stat-check-verifies" tabindex="-1">The watcher discovers and the stat check verifies <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-watcher-discovers-and-the-stat-check-verifies" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The watcher discovers and the stat check verifies&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Issue #136 needs the Language server to hold the same bytes the agent just wrote. pi edits files on disk and holds no buffer, and an agent moves files in ways pi&#39;s edit tools never see: a Python script with a search-and-replace loop, <code>sed</code>, a build that regenerates sources, a codemode script, or another agent in a sibling worktree. Disk is the only truth, so Document sync cannot be built on mirroring pi&#39;s own edits.</p><p><strong>A recursive file watcher is the discovery mechanism and a stat check is the correctness gate.</strong> One watcher per served root records the Changed set: which paths moved since the last pass. Before any answer is served from a document, its mtime and size are checked against the record and the document is re-synced when they moved. The watcher decides what to look at; it never decides what is current.</p><p><strong>A watcher event is not proof.</strong> Events can be dropped on queue overflow, coalesced, delivered before the writer finishes, or missed for an atomic temp-plus-rename save. codegraph already runs this exact pairing on the same roots: it watches, and it reconciles, and it reconciles before every query when the watcher is degraded. This extension copies that policy, including the 1000 ms debounce, the WSL2 <code>/mnt</code> rule, and an env opt-out (<code>LSP_NO_WATCH=1</code>).</p><p><strong>Considered options.</strong> Stat-only (no watcher) needs a <code>git status</code> sweep to find files changed outside the Open documents, which is wrong in a non-git directory and pays a subprocess per turn. Watcher-only was the tempting answer to &quot;one mechanism solves both&quot;, and it is the one that can serve an answer from a half-written file. Reusing codegraph&#39;s watcher through <code>pi.events</code> would run one watcher per root for the whole package, but codegraph&#39;s callbacks report change counts rather than paths, and it would make the LSP extension depend on codegraph being loaded and watching. That option is recorded in the spec as the thing to revisit once both extensions exist.</p><p><strong>Consequences.</strong> When the watcher degrades, the extension falls back to the stat pass over the Open documents plus a bounded <code>git status</code> sweep, so it never loses the ability to see a change, it only pays more for it. The watcher&#39;s scope is the roots the extension actually serves, never the home directory, and it excludes <code>.git</code>, <code>node_modules</code>, <code>target</code>, <code>build</code>, <code>dist</code>, <code>.venv</code>, <code>__pycache__</code>, and <code>.codegraph</code>. A change event for a file the server does not have open only enters the Changed set: opening a document makes the server analyze it, so files outside the agent&#39;s working set are not opened until the Sweep decides they are worth the cap.</p>',6)])])}const g=t(n,[["render",r]]);export{u as __pageData,g as default};
